course: dictionary
lesson: 7967
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Recital is one of them. You may know it as a show where pupils play music or dance for their parents, and that meaning is right. But it can also mean a telling of many things, one after another.
explain: Toad is excited by «praise and admiration at the recital of his adventures». Praise and admiration are feelings people have when they hear something, so the recital must be a telling. It is not a concert. The clue is in the words around it.
explain: Ask yourself: does the everyday meaning fit the sentence? Try it. A piano recital fits a music show, but it makes no sense when Toad is praised for his adventures. Then try the other meaning, and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: A recital is a performance of music, dance or poetry, often by pupils, for an audience.
fact: A recital can also be a spoken list or account of many things, one after another, as in «a recital of complaints».
fact: Recitation is the act of saying a poem or text aloud from memory.
fact: A piano recital or dance recital is a show where pupils perform what they have learned for parents.
fact: «Recital» is a noun: one recital, two recitals.
exercise: «The end-of-year recital started at six o'clock.» What is a recital here? | *A show where pupils perform for an audience | A type of car | A loud argument | A long list of complaints
exercise: «She gave a long recital of every mistake we had made.» What does recital mean here? | *A telling of many things, one after another | A quiet whisper | A dance on stage | A piano concert
exercise: Which phrase means a show where pupils perform what they have learned? | barely a whisper | *dance recital | glum mood | treacherous path
test: «He gave a recital of facts that went on for an hour.» What does recital suggest? | Music played loudly | *A long telling of facts, one after another | A dance with a partner | A quiet whisper
test: Mum says, «Your recital starts at seven.» What is the likeliest meaning? | A lost bag | A list of groceries | *A performance of music or dance for an audience | A broken clock
exam: Ravi says a recital is only ever a concert. Is he right? | *No, it can also mean a telling of many things, one after another | No, it means a whisper | Yes, but only for dancing | Yes, it is only ever a concert
